---
import BaseLayoutEn from '../../layouts/BaseLayoutEn.astro';
import { siteEn } from '../../data/site-en';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL === '/' ? '' : import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const withBase = (path: string) => `${base}/${path.replace(/^\/+/, '')}`;
---

<BaseLayoutEn
  title={`Founder of the Institute | ${siteEn.shortName}`}
  description="A page about Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko, founder and first director of the S.P. Timoshenko Institute of Mechanics of the NAS of Ukraine."
  activePage="about"
>
  <section class="page-hero founder-hero">
    <div class="container founder-page-container">
      <div class="panel founder-hero-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">About the Institute</p>
        <h1>S.P. Timoshenko — Founder of the Institute of Mechanics</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko was an outstanding scientist in mechanics of worldwide renown,
          one of the founders of the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences, and the founder and first director
          of the Institute of Mechanics of the NAS of Ukraine, which now bears his name.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section founder-section">
    <div class="container founder-page-container founder-container">
      <article class="panel founder-article">
        <section class="founder-content-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">The beginning of a scientific path</p>
          <h2>The formation of S.P. Timoshenko as a scientist in mechanics and educator</h2>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-portrait">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/01_tymoshenko_portrait.jpeg')} alt="Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko (1878–1972) was an outstanding scientist in mechanics
            of worldwide renown, a prominent organiser of science and a distinguished educator.
            Professor S.P. Timoshenko entered the history of Ukrainian science as one of the founders
            of the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences, now the National Academy of Sciences of Ukraine,
            and as the founder and first director of the Institute of Mechanics of the NAS of Ukraine,
            which now bears his name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko was born on 23 December 1878 in the village of Shpotivka,
            Konotop county, Chernihiv province, now Sumy region, into the family of the land surveyor
            Prokip Tymofiiovych Timoshenko and his wife Yuzefina Yakivna Sarnavska, the daughter
            of a retired Polish military officer.
          </p>

          <p>
            In 1896 he graduated from a vocational school in the town of Romny and entered the Institute
            of Railway Engineers in St Petersburg. This was a prestigious technical educational institution,
            founded in 1809, where mathematics, mechanics and thermodynamics were taught by such well-known
            professors as D.O. Grave, D.I. Zhuravsky, M.A. Belelyubsky, O.M. Krylov and O.A. Brandt.
            After graduating in 1901, he worked at this institute until 1906, as well as at the St Petersburg
            Polytechnic Institute, studying, in particular, resonance phenomena in shafts, the stability
            of the plane form of beam bending, buckling of rods in elastic media, and stress concentration
            in joints. During this period he undertook a scientific visit to France in 1901 and, in 1904–1906,
            attended lectures and trained in Germany at the universities of Munich and Göttingen under
            the renowned scientists in mechanics A. Föppl (1854–1924) and L. Prandtl (1875–1953).
          </p>

          <p>
            While working at the St Petersburg Polytechnic Institute, S.P. Timoshenko became acquainted
            with Viktor Lvovych Kirpichov (1845–1913), a well-known specialist in strength of materials
            and the founder of engineering schools at the Kharkiv Technological Institute and the Kyiv
            Polytechnic Institute. According to Timoshenko himself, Kirpichov had a decisive influence
            on his scientific and teaching career. It was Kirpichov, the first rector of the Kyiv Polytechnic
            Institute in 1898–1902, who advised the young scientist to compete for the vacant professorship
            at the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute. Having been elected to this position, S.P. Timoshenko moved
            to Kyiv in 1906. The following year he defended his dissertation and received the academic degree
            of adjunct in applied mechanics. Soon he became head of the Department of Strength of Materials,
            and in 1908 secretary and later dean of the mechanical and civil engineering faculties (1909–1911).
            However, after signing a protest in January 1911 against police arbitrariness, he was dismissed
            together with other professors.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-document">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/11_additional_image.jpg')} alt="Letter from S.P. Timoshenko to the Director of the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              Letter from S.P. Timoshenko to V.F. Tymofieiev, Director of the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute,
              accepting the position of extraordinary professor at the Department of Strength of Materials
              (from the materials of the State Archive of the City of Kyiv).
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            This relatively short first period of work at the Kyiv Polytechnic nevertheless proved very
            fruitful for the scientist. He completely changed the teaching of strength of materials and
            the theory of elasticity and prepared fundamental textbooks on the theory of vibrations,
            the theory of stability of deformable systems, engineering mechanics, applied dynamics,
            and the theory of plates and shells. Timoshenko studied the stability of plates, circular rings
            and compressed slabs, the influence of circular holes on the stress distribution in plates,
            and forced vibrations of prismatic rods. One result of his scientific work was the monograph
            On the Stability of Elastic Systems, published in 1910 and awarded the D.I. Zhuravsky Prize
            “for outstanding works in structural mechanics”; problems of stability of elastic systems were
            also considered in his German-language paper.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-wide">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/02_kpi_department_1908.png')} alt="Staff of the Department of Strength of Materials of the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute, 1908" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              Staff of the Department of Strength of Materials of the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute. From left
              to right, first row: lecturers E.K. Garf, S.P. Timoshenko, M.O. Voropaiev; second row:
              O.M. Dynnyk, M.I. Kohan, M.S. Ruzhytskyi; third row: laboratory assistants M.V. Skorokhod
              and L.I. Lunakevych. 1908.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            After his dismissal from the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute, Stepan Prokopovych returned to
            St Petersburg, where he later held professorial positions at the Polytechnic Institute,
            the Electrotechnical Institute and the Institute of Railway Engineers. He was also involved
            as a scientific consultant in the construction of warships and undertook a scientific visit
            to Great Britain.
          </p>

          <p>
            During these years he carried out research on the strength of steam turbines, deformation
            and stability of cylindrical and spherical shells, bending and torsion of prismatic rods,
            vibration and dynamic stresses in railway rails, and impact loads on beams. He published
            a number of recognised scientific works, including abroad, and created the textbooks
            Course of Strength of Materials (1911) and Course of the Theory of Elasticity (1914–1916),
            which became classics and were repeatedly republished and translated into English. His teaching
            experience inspired him to develop the idea of a new model of technical education. Together
            with other scientists, he prepared a curriculum and a project for the creation of an engineering
            and research physics-mechanics faculty within the St Petersburg Polytechnic Institute, an institution
            that at that time had no analogue in the world.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="founder-content-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">Ukrainian Academy of Sciences</p>
          <h2>S.P. Timoshenko’s role in organising the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences and the Institute of Technical Mechanics</h2>

          <p>
            Thus, when he returned to Kyiv at the end of 1917, after the Bolshevik coup in Petrograd,
            at the invitation of the council of professors of the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute to take up
            a professorship there, S.P. Timoshenko already had a reputation not only as a well-known scientist
            in mechanics and educator, but also as an expert on the organisation of scientific research
            in mechanics in the leading countries of Western Europe.
          </p>

          <p>
            These factors were evidently decisive for Mykola Prokopovych Vasylenko (1866–1935), Minister
            of Public Education and Arts in the Hetman government of P. Skoropadskyi and a scholar of law,
            and for Volodymyr Ivanovych Vernadsky (1863–1945), the renowned geologist and geochemist,
            member of the Russian Academy of Sciences, who in early summer 1918 invited Professor Timoshenko
            to work in the Commission for the Organisation of the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-wide">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/03_vasylenko_vernadsky.png')} alt="Mykola Prokopovych Vasylenko and Volodymyr Ivanovych Vernadsky" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              Mykola Prokopovych Vasylenko, Minister of Public Education and Arts of the Ukrainian State
              and a renowned legal scholar; Volodymyr Ivanovych Vernadsky, member of the Russian Academy
              of Sciences and an outstanding natural scientist.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            According to Academician Vernadsky’s concept, the Academy was to become the country’s main
            scientific centre, uniting research institutions across a broad range of technical, natural,
            social and human sciences. Vernadsky was convinced that only a state-based concept of Academy
            development would make it possible to form a strong scientific base and national scientific personnel.
            His ideas were grounded in a vision of future social, economic and technological progress and,
            accordingly, of the growing role of science as a productive force in developing the foundations
            of the state and its economic strength. Alongside the fundamental fields of the natural sciences,
            Vernadsky placed a “division of applied natural science”, where knowledge related to technology,
            industry, agriculture, forestry and water management was to be developed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stepan Prokopovych was among the few representatives of the natural and technical fields
            in the Commission who laid the foundations of the future Academy, developed the conceptual basis
            for its natural-science and technical directions, and stood at the head of the first academic
            institutes and departments. The Commission worked from July to October 1918.
          </p>

          <p>
            On 22 July, S.P. Timoshenko delivered a keynote report on the organisation of applied natural
            science within the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences. In it he noted, in particular, that “the times
            when science and technology followed separate paths have already passed; and today, very often,
            to solve purely technical problems, one uses the powerful instrument provided by mathematics
            and mechanics”. Timoshenko proposed organising, within the Second, Physico-Mathematical Department,
            a class of applied natural science, thereby bringing together science and technology to fulfil
            the main tasks of developing science in Ukraine, studying the natural resources of the country,
            and developing and improving methods for their use.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the same report, Stepan Prokopovych pointed to the important role of the Academy in training
            professional personnel and professors for higher education in the technical sciences. He stated that
            the question of training professors had long been placed on the agenda by life itself: universities
            and higher technical schools constantly felt a shortage of properly trained professors, and many
            departments remained vacant from year to year. In Ukraine this issue would become even more urgent,
            since a number of new higher schools were planned, while no ready professors were available for them.
            It was therefore natural, in his view, to entrust the training of future professors to the Academy
            of Sciences.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the Commission meeting on 24 July, he reported on the expediency of granting the Academy
            the right to train highly qualified scientific personnel and award academic degrees, including
            the right to confer the degree of Doctor of Sciences for outstanding scientific work, a prerogative
            previously held by universities. This would allow the Academy to replenish its own ranks with
            specialists in fundamental and applied sciences.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timoshenko also considered the possibility of creating applied chairs within the Physico-Mathematical
            Department, including a chair of technical mechanics with laboratories of applied mechanics and
            structural mechanics. These could bring together such subjects as kinematics and dynamics of machines,
            steamships, railway engineering and aviation, hydraulics and hydrodynamics with applications,
            aerodynamics, the theory of elasticity, strength of materials, structural statics as applied
            to the calculation of bridges, roofs, vaults and beams, as well as problems of the strength
            of ships and aeroplanes. The laboratory base was to be united in the Institute of Technical Mechanics,
            where departments of applied mechanics and structural mechanics were to be opened.
          </p>

          <p>
            On 4 September, the Commission discussed proposals to include in the Academy’s Statute
            a prospective list of scientific institutions, in particular an Astronomical Laboratory,
            a Botanical Garden, a Geodetic Institute, a Geological Committee and the Institute of Technical Mechanics.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-document">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/04_hetman_order_1918.png')} alt="Order of Hetman Pavlo Skoropadskyi of 14 November 1918" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              Order of Hetman of All Ukraine P. Skoropadskyi of 14 November 1918 appointing
              the first academicians of the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-document">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/05_letter_uas_president_secretary_1918.jpeg')} alt="Letter from Academicians O.I. Levytskyi and S.P. Timoshenko to Minister of Public Education V.P. Naumenko" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              Letter from Academicians O.I. Levytskyi and S.P. Timoshenko to Minister of Public Education
              V.P. Naumenko concerning the election of the President-Chairman and Permanent Secretary
              of the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The work of the Commission ended with the adoption, on 14 November 1918, of the Law of the
            Ukrainian State on the establishment of the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences in Kyiv, signed by
            Hetman of All Ukraine Pavlo Skoropadskyi. On the same day, by order of the Hetman, the first
            twelve full members of the Academy were appointed; among them was the youngest academician,
            the 39-year-old S.P. Timoshenko.
          </p>

          <p>
            On 27 November 1918, the beginning of the full functioning of the Academy was marked by its
            first constitutive General Assembly. At the meeting, chaired by the oldest academician,
            O.I. Levytskyi, and with the youngest academician, S.P. Timoshenko, serving as secretary,
            Academician V.I. Vernadsky was unanimously elected President-Chairman, and Academician
            A.Yu. Krymskyi Permanent Secretary. That same day, Academicians O.I. Levytskyi and
            S.P. Timoshenko informed Minister of Public Education of the Ukrainian State V.P. Naumenko
            by letter of the election of the President-Chairman and Permanent Secretary; on 30 November 1918,
            the Hetman issued an order appointing Academician V.I. Vernadsky President-Chairman of the UAS.
            The General Assembly elected M.T. Kashchenko as head of the Physico-Mathematical Department,
            while S.P. Timoshenko became secretary of the Department, a position he conscientiously held
            until his departure abroad in early 1920.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thanks to the organisational energy and personal qualities of Academician S.P. Timoshenko,
            the Institute of Technical Mechanics was created as one of the first institutes of the Academy.
            This happened on 30 November 1918 at the second General Assembly of the UAS, where Stepan
            Prokopovych was approved as director of the Institute. At the same meeting, Academician
            Timoshenko was elected head of the Chair of Applied Mechanics and head of the Section
            of Construction Materials.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="founder-content-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">The first years of the Institute</p>
          <h2>Scientific and organisational activity of S.P. Timoshenko in the Academy</h2>

          <p>
            The Academy’s report for 1919 noted S.P. Timoshenko’s major scientific and organisational
            contribution, including the creation of institutes, chairs and laboratories, the development
            of publishing activity for scientific works, and the search for premises, materials and funds
            for acquiring laboratory equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Under his leadership, from the beginning of 1919 a scientific seminar on theoretical questions
            of a technical nature began its work, and the publication Proceedings of the Institute of Technical
            Mechanics was launched. Important studies were conducted by S.P. Timoshenko and E.K. Garf on
            the strength of reinforced concrete beams under variable loads and on the strength of a spherical
            film under uniform pressure. At that time Stepan Prokopovych wrote his works On the Strength
            of Aeroplanes and Calculation of Arches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Developing the idea of integrating science and technology, Academician S.P. Timoshenko, as director
            of the Institute of Technical Mechanics, initiated and chaired the Council on Construction Materials.
            Its responsibilities included approving standards for various construction materials, programmes
            of relevant research work, reports of the testing station and related tasks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timoshenko proposed that the Council include two heads of departments of the testing station,
            the director of the Institute of Applied Physics, the director of the Geological Committee,
            an academician from the chair of geology, two academicians elected by the Second Department,
            representatives of the ministries of transport, trade and industry, land affairs, the military
            and naval ministries, and three representatives from the Kyiv Polytechnic, Kharkiv Technological
            and Katerynoslav Mining Institutes.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-portrait">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/06_tymoshenko_brothers.png')} alt="S.P. Timoshenko with his brothers Serhii and Volodymyr" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              S.P. Timoshenko (centre) with his brothers Serhii (left) and Volodymyr (right).
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Summing up the selfless work of Academician S.P. Timoshenko in the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences,
            it should be noted that Stepan Prokopovych was one of V.I. Vernadsky’s like-minded colleagues
            and together with him laid the foundations for the Academy’s future achievements. With his active
            participation, the Physico-Mathematical Department acquired priority importance, while he himself
            became head of the Academy’s first research institution — the Institute of Technical Mechanics —
            and created the prerequisites for its further effective activity. Thus, S.P. Timoshenko can rightly
            be regarded as the founder of the development of research in mechanics at the National Academy
            of Sciences of Ukraine.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth noting that during the years of the Ukrainian Revolution of 1917–1920,
            Stepan Prokopovych’s brothers — Serhii Prokopovych, an architect by profession, and Volodymyr
            Prokopovych, an economist by profession — were prominent public and political figures and statesmen
            of the Ukrainian People’s Republic and actively participated in the development of Ukrainian statehood.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="founder-content-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">A worldwide scientific school</p>
          <h2>Life and work of S.P. Timoshenko in emigration</h2>

          <p>
            Due to the political and economic situation that arose after the arrival in Kyiv first of Denikin’s
            forces and then of the Bolsheviks, the activity of the UAS was effectively suspended. This forced
            S.P. Timoshenko in March 1920 to decide to leave the country.
          </p>

          <p>
            At first he headed the Department of Strength of Materials at the Zagreb Polytechnic Institute,
            and in 1922 accepted an invitation to move to the United States. For his first five years there
            he worked in Philadelphia in the research department of the Westinghouse company, where, among
            other things, he organised evening seminars to improve the qualifications of engineers in technical
            mechanics. In 1927 he moved to academic teaching.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-portrait">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/07_michigan_university_1936.png')} alt="S.P. Timoshenko among faculty and students of the University of Michigan, 1936" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>S.P. Timoshenko among faculty and students of the University of Michigan. 1936.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            He became head of the Department of Applied Mechanics at the University of Michigan, where in 1929
            he opened a summer school in mechanics for graduate specialists, which was of great significance
            for the development of this science and for the training of specialists in the United States.
            There he gave special lecture courses through which hundreds of engineers deepened their knowledge.
            Under his supervision about forty doctoral dissertations were defended. At that time Professor
            S.P. Timoshenko also organised a mechanics section within the American Society of Mechanical Engineers.
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-wide">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/08_stanford_laboratory_1939.png')} alt="S.P. Timoshenko in the Engineering Mechanics Laboratory at Stanford University, 1939" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>S.P. Timoshenko in the Engineering Mechanics Laboratory at Stanford University. 1939.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            From 1936, for a quarter of a century, the scientist taught at Stanford University: until 1943
            he was head of the Department of Mechanics and then, until 1960, its professor. There he developed
            bachelor’s and doctoral programmes in engineering mechanics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stepan Prokopovych devoted more than sixty years to the development of engineering science and
            education in different countries. In the final years of his scientific and teaching activity,
            he travelled extensively, visiting universities and scientific laboratories in Germany, Great Britain,
            France, Italy, Switzerland and other Western European countries, giving lectures and reports, and
            collecting materials for his well-known book on the history of strength of materials. Having retired
            in 1960, from 1964 he lived with his daughter Hanna in Wuppertal, Germany. S.P. Timoshenko died
            on 29 May 1972 and was buried next to his wife in Palo Alto, USA.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thanks to his scientific and engineering talent and exceptional capacity for work, S.P. Timoshenko
            made an enormous contribution to world science. His scientific works became the basis for the
            development of numerous areas of mechanics. For many years he held a leading position among
            specialists in mechanics in the United States.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a letter to V.I. Vernadsky written as early as 1923, Stepan Prokopovych emphasised the almost
            unlimited opportunities that were opening in the United States for research in mechanics, oriented
            primarily toward applied use. This enabled the scientist to realise his research ideas. It is no
            coincidence that, opening the Twelfth International Congress of Applied Mechanics held in August 1968
            at Stanford University, Congress President Professor N.J. Hoff noted that S.P. Timoshenko had done
            very much for the development of applied mechanics, particularly in the United States of America.
          </p>

          <p>
            His contribution was especially great in the development of applied theory of elasticity,
            the theory of stability of shell and plate systems, including those reinforced by stiffeners.
            Important studies by S.P. Timoshenko concerned bending, torsion, vibration and impact of structural
            elements. He solved a number of problems related to stress concentration near holes and the strength
            of railway rails under dynamic loads. In the theory of thin-walled systems, the so-called Timoshenko-type
            model, which takes shear deformations into account, became widely used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professor Timoshenko studied deeply the history of the science of strength of materials and made
            a major contribution to the formation of engineering education. He created a series of unique monographs
            and textbooks in various fields of engineering mechanics, translated into many languages and used to
            educate many generations of specialists in mechanics.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="founder-content-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">Commemoration</p>
          <h2>Honouring the memory of the outstanding scientist in Ukraine and worldwide</h2>

          <p>
            Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko visited Ukraine twice, in 1958 and 1967, and visited scientific
            institutions and higher educational establishments in Kyiv, Lviv and Kharkiv. During his last trip,
            he also visited the town of Romny in Sumy region, where he had received his primary education.
            Recalling his 1958 visit to the Institute of Mechanics, which he had founded, he wrote:
            “The inspection of the mechanics laboratory gave me great satisfaction. The main idea of bringing
            science and technology closer together, which had so inspired me when organising the Chair of Mechanics
            at the Kyiv Academy of Sciences, proved viable, and the laboratory I had planned is actively participating
            in solving important technical problems of the country.”
          </p>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-wide">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/09_institute_meeting_1958.png')} alt="Meeting of S.P. Timoshenko with the research staff of the Institute of Structural Mechanics, 1958" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              Meeting of S.P. Timoshenko with the research staff of the Institute of Structural Mechanics. 1958.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure class="founder-figure founder-figure-portrait">
            <img src={withBase('/assets/img/10_romny_trip_1967.png')} alt="S.P. Timoshenko and H.S. Pisarenko during a trip to Romny, 1967" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'" />
            <figcaption>
              S.P. Timoshenko (right) and H.S. Pisarenko resting during a trip to Romny. 1967.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The outstanding scientific achievements of S.P. Timoshenko were recognised throughout the world.
            He was elected a member of the Ukrainian (1918), Polish (1935), French (1939) and Italian (1948)
            academies of sciences, the Academy of Sciences of the USSR (1928), and the Royal Society of London
            (1944). He was also a full member of the Shevchenko Scientific Society (1924) and an honorary doctor
            of many universities.
          </p>

          <p>
            In 1957, the American Society of Mechanical Engineers established the S.P. Timoshenko Medal,
            which he was the first to receive “for his inestimable contributions and personal example as leader
            of a new era in applied mechanics”.
          </p>

          <p>
            The outstanding contribution of Academician S.P. Timoshenko to Ukrainian and world science has
            also been honoured in Ukraine. In 1993, his name was given to the Institute of Mechanics of the
            NAS of Ukraine, and in 1999 the Academy established the S.P. Timoshenko Prize for outstanding
            achievements in theoretical and applied mechanics. In 1998, a monument to Stepan Prokopovych was
            erected on the grounds of the Kyiv Polytechnic Institute. A memorial plaque was also installed
            on building No. 23 on Hoholivska Street in Kyiv, where he lived in 1917–1920.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stepan Prokopovych Timoshenko belongs to the cohort of outstanding sons of the Ukrainian people
            who, through their talent and tireless creative work, glorified Ukrainian science and Ukraine
            throughout the world. His unsurpassed achievements in scientific, organisational and pedagogical work
            continue, and will continue, to serve as a vivid example for new generations of researchers.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="founder-content-section founder-bibliography">
          <p class="eyebrow">Bibliography</p>
          <h2>Selected publications by S.P. Timoshenko</h2>

